<div style="text-align: center; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Fun Outdoors! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love to Run!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple responses to texts. Interactive: Write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can respond to short texts by writing simple sentences (e.g., "I like this story about running."). Can write simple messages to peers using words, drawings, and short phrases about outdoor activities (e.g., drawing a soccer ball and writing "Let's play!"). Can express simple reactions to texts using words or phrases (e.g., "Fun story!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of outdoor activities (running, swimming, playing soccer, jumping). Students say the word aloud. Teacher quickly writes one word on the board, e.g., "run." Then the teacher mimes the action and says, "I love to run!" and students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "I run fast." and points to each word as it is said. Then writes "I love to play soccer." and models its pronunciation, emphasizing /r/ in "run" and /s/ in "swim" if those words are used. The teacher circles "run" and "fast" and explains their meaning with gestures and simple examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "run fast" and asks, "Am I moving slowly or quickly?" (Expected answer: "Quickly!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "I love to play soccer." and asks, "Do I like soccer a little or a lot?" (Expected answer: "A lot!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated narrative text (e.g., "My Day at the Park") displayed on a projector or as a handout. The text describes children doing outdoor activities with simple sentences like: "My friend Lily and I go to the park. We love to run fast! My friend Tom plays soccer. He kicks the ball quickly. It is fun! We like to jump high on the playground." (CEFR: Pre-A1.4 appropriate content).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will learn to express preferences for activities ("I like to run. It's fun.") and observe how characters ask and answer questions about others' preferences ("Do you like to play outside?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to words. Students then circle all the "action words" (verbs) they find in the text (e.g., run, play, kick, jump).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match pictures of outdoor activities (e.g., a child running, a child playing soccer) to the corresponding written sentence from the text on a worksheet.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple questions like "Who loves to run fast?" Students write the name from the text (e.g., "Lily").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on writing sentences using "Subject + verb + adverb" (e.g., "I run fast," "I swim slowly") and "Present simple" to express preferences (e.g., "I love to run"). Vocabulary: run, jump, swim, play, fast, slow, happy, fun, ball, park, friend.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with incomplete sentences and a word bank. Students choose words to complete sentences. Example: "I like to ____ ____." (Word bank: run, fast, play, happy, swim, slowly). "My friend ____ in the ____." (Word bank: plays, park, ball).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Transformation):</b> The teacher writes a sentence on the board, e.g., "I play soccer." Students are asked to rewrite the sentence, adding an adverb or changing the verb/noun. Teacher provides prompts: "Add how you play." (Expected: "I play soccer quickly.") or "Change 'soccer' to 'basketball'." (Expected: "I play basketball."). The teacher guides them to write "I play basketball fun!" or "I play basketball. It's fun."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Practice - Guided Writing):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a picture of an outdoor activity (e.g., a child running, a child riding a bike, children playing a game). They write one simple sentence about their picture using the target grammar (e.g., "I run fast." or "He rides a bike slowly.") and one sentence expressing preference (e.g., "It is fun!"). They then exchange pictures and sentences with their partner, reading each other's sentences.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Outdoor Activity Message Card." Students will create a small message card for a peer, combining drawing and simple written messages.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a blank card. On one side, students draw their favorite outdoor activity (e.g., playing soccer, running in the park, swimming).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">On the other side of the card, students write a simple message to a friend about the activity. They must include:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their name.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The name of the activity.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One sentence expressing preference (e.g., "I love to play!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One sentence describing an action with an adverb (e.g., "I run fast!").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides sentence starters on the board: "To my friend ____," "I love to ____ ____." "I ____ ____." "It's ____!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students exchange their message cards with a classmate and read the message. They can respond verbally with a simple reaction like "Wow!" or "Fun!" or a simple written word like "Cool!".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Outdoor Activity Message Card" task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' completed "Outdoor Activity Message Cards."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes at least one sentence using Subject + verb (e.g., "I play.") (1 point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least one target vocabulary word from the theme (e.g., run, play, fast, fun) (1 point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes a sentence expressing preference (e.g., "I love to play!") (1 point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort and legibility (1 point)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write one word about my favorite game. Yes / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a simple sentence about playing outside. Yes / No"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about playing outside did I write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about writing messages to my friends?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson for 2nd graders? (e.g., picture prompts, sentence starters, peer interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with writing simple sentences or using adverbs? (e.g., spelling, word order).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support their writing development? (e.g., more direct modeling of adverb placement, simplified vocabulary choices).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5, students will integrate their understanding of outdoor activities and preferences by sharing their written messages, summarizing information about their friends' favorite activities, and preparing for a short presentation or display of their work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>